@meta
id: 05-07
title: Drill — past the chain with W
chapter: 05
type: drill
skills: WORD-motions, word-counts
par: 18
minutes: 4
reps: 8
vscode: clicking into the middle of the update call
open: order.repository.ts

@brief
Eight times: add `total` to the values object so the update writes
`{ total, status }`. The cursor has to cross a repository chain and two
braces to get there.

Use `W` and count the stops. Par is set so that the `w` route does not
fit.

@keys
| `W` | next WORD, whitespace separated |
| `8W` | eight of them |
| `i` | insert before the cursor |

@hints
The cursor starts on `return`. The stops from there are the whole
`this.orderRepository.update({` chunk, `where:`, `{`, `id`, `},`,
`values:`, `{`, `status`. Eight.
--
Count once, then stop counting. By rep four you should be pressing `8W`
because the shape of the line is familiar, not because you recounted it.

@solution
    8W  i total,  <Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Fifteen keystrokes. With `w` the same landing is fifteen presses of one
key, which is slower to do and, worse, impossible to check — you cannot
tell by eye whether you pressed it fourteen or sixteen times.

That is the real argument for the count: a number you worked out once is
verifiable. A key you held down is not.

@start order.repository.ts
    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status } });

@expected order.repository.ts
    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { total, status } });
